  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 178,
      "text": "MEXICO\n46. The supply side policy agenda should be broadened to unlock Mexico’s growth\npotential. The current administration’s policy agenda has focused on redistribution, trade\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 178,
      "end_char": 366,
      "text": "promotion, infrastructure investment, and trade integration to foster development, especially in\nSouthern states. Flagship reforms include (i) the implementation of the U.S.-Mexico-Canada\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 366,
      "end_char": 560,
      "text": "Agreement (USMCA); (ii) increases in the minimum wage and the subcontracting law to reduce wage\ninequality; (iii) increases in the general social pension to reduce old age poverty; and (iv) the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 560,
      "end_char": 749,
      "text": "development of trade infrastructure in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec (“Isthmus corridor”) in the South\nof Mexico. While this agenda addresses some obstacles to higher productivity and growth,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 749,
      "end_char": 948,
      "text": "additional efforts are needed. Priorities include corruption, crime and rule of law issues; addressing\nhuman capital and infrastructure shortfalls; and lessening labor and product market rigidities.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 948,
      "end_char": 1168,
      "text": "A Budget for Higher, More Equitable Growth\n47. A pivot to a more equitable and pro-growth budget would entail a gradual increase in\nproductive government spending, financed by revenue-increasing tax reforms. To lift the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1168,
      "end_char": 1355,
      "text": "growth potential and reduce socioeconomic gaps, Mexico should address spending shortfalls in\neducation, health, public investment, and social safety nets (IMF Staff Report 2021/2020; IMF\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1355,
      "end_char": 1546,
      "text": "Selected Issues 2019, Paper 2; IMF WP/20/215). Structural indicators such as spending per student,\nthe teacher-to-student ratio, and public health spending per capita are well below the OECD\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1546,
      "end_char": 1735,
      "text": "averages and somewhat below emerging market averages (IMF WP/21/244). The pandemic has\nfurther increased spending needs, particularly in education (following 18 months of school closures).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1735,
      "end_char": 1923,
      "text": "Separately, there is scope, and a need, to increase revenue through tax reform, to support the\nadditional spending and secure tax buoyancy.12\n48. A gradual, permanent Growth Decomposition\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1923,
      "end_char": 2159,
      "text": "increase in spending of around 2 (Annual averages in percent)\n4\nto 3 percent of GDP could foster\n3\ninclusive growth, reduce\n2\npoverty, and address pandemic\n1\nlegacies. This feasible step would\n0\nhelp make progress in achieving\n-1 Labor\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2159,
      "end_char": 2397,
      "text": "the Social Development Goals Capital\n-2 Total factor productivity\n(SDG; IMF WP/21/244). The gains Total GDP\n-3\nfrom higher spending on\neducation, health, public\ninvestment, and social safety nets\nSources:INEGI and IMF staff calculations.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2397,
      "end_char": 2621,
      "text": "could be enhanced with more\nefficiency in programs. For example, social assistance could reduce the leakage of benefits to high-\nincome groups and eliminate overlaps and coverage gaps with the creation of single beneficiary\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2621,
      "end_char": 2850,
      "text": "12 Based on staff’s macroeconomic forecasts, there is a fiscal gap of 0.2 percent of GDP in 2023, which gradually\nincreases to around 1.3 percent of GDP by 2027. In recent years, the authorities have closed expected gaps through\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2850,
      "end_char": 3055,
      "text": "revenue and spending measures and small changes in the budget deficit target. The revenues generated from staff’s\nproposed tax reforms could also be used to close these gaps.\n24 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 30",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 199,
      "text": "MEXICO\nscheme would be needed for those who are most negatively affected by the resulting energy\nprice increase.\n51. Overall, the comprehensive fiscal package of higher spending financed with higher\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 199,
      "end_char": 365,
      "text": "revenues should have positive complementarities between growth and equity.\n• The higher permanent spending aims to close above-discussed socioeconomic gaps that have\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 365,
      "end_char": 546,
      "text": "held back investment in human capital and productivity. In the context of Mexico where the\nsocioeconomic gaps are concentrated in specific regions, the increased and more efficient\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 546,
      "end_char": 735,
      "text": "productive spending, especially in those regions, should enhance both growth and equity.\n• The trade-offs between growth and increased revenues are likely to be small as a significant part\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 735,
      "end_char": 913,
      "text": "come from VAT reforms, which should not distort factor supplies or affect long-term growth\nespecially since the reforms would be accompanied by compensation for poor households.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 913,
      "end_char": 1151,
      "text": "Increases in the personal income tax would largely be through simplifying and expanding the\ntax base, which would have minimal effects on labor supply.\n• Together, such budget reforms would increase growth and place public debt on a firm\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1151,
      "end_char": 1360,
      "text": "downward trajectory over the medium term (IMF Staff Report 2021, IMF Staff Report 2020, IMF\nWP/20/215).\nAuthorities’ Views\n52. They are supportive of a pro-growth productive spending increase supported by tax\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1360,
      "end_char": 1551,
      "text": "reforms. But note such measures would require time to be implemented. In the meantime, they\nremain confident that the administrative measures to combat tax evasion and tax fraud taken by the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1551,
      "end_char": 1722,
      "text": "current administration would continue to maintain robust revenues.\nOther Structural Policies\n53. A determined implementation of the anticorruption framework would help in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1722,
      "end_char": 1913,
      "text": "addressing corruption and other governance issues. Contract enforcement by civil justice is very\nweak, discouraging business expansion and formal employment. This also complicates collateral\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1913,
      "end_char": 2102,
      "text": "seizure and impedes financial development. Corruption undermines the provision on public services.\nOrganized crime increases transport and insurance costs and discourages entrepreneurs and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2102,
      "end_char": 2289,
      "text": "business formation. Improving governance would unlock investment and growth potential. A new\nlaw treating corruption and fraud as felony offenses is expected to enable more comprehensive\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2289,
      "end_char": 2495,
      "text": "investigations on corruption. The challenge lies in implementing and assessing effectiveness of the\nanti-corruption policies, and in further coordinating state levels. Prevention, facilitation of reporting\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2495,
      "end_char": 2687,
      "text": "including with whistleblowing protection and further empowering the institutions in charge of\ninvestigation, prosecution and oversight can improve implementation. Mexico should participate in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2687,
      "end_char": 2809,
      "text": "the next round of voluntary assessments of transnational aspects of corruption by the Fund.\n26 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
